pping the denominators first and then multiplying the resulting fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21}{20}\\times \\frac{10}{1} & =\\frac{21}{1}\\times \\frac{10}{20} \\\\  & =21\\times \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21\\times 1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{2}=10\\,\\text{R}\\,1=10\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433031 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1069 "q-211195" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction or mixed number in its lowest terms, $4\\times 1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=${{field-1}}." 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1072 #uuid "83a10579-467e-5020-9446-f0bcbebf0762" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433038 1074 "First, we write $4$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n4=\\frac{4}{1}\n$$\n\nWe also write $1\\,\\frac{3}{5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{(1\\times 5)+3}{5}=\\frac{8}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{1}\\times \\frac{8}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 8}{1\\times 5}=\\frac{32}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can also write this improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{5}=6\\,\\text{R}\\,2=6\\,\\frac{2}{5}\n$$" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433033 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1069 "q-211257" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1071 "$1\\,\\frac{1}{8}\\times 8=$" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1072 #uuid "8cf2b8e2-2314-5e9d-b171-6805247e3cfc" 13194139534693 17592186045417 true] [17592186433039 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\frac{1}{8}$ as